WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.969
Film Courage: What are your five worst screenwriting
habits?

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.530
Mark Sanderson: Five worst?

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:13.730
Well, it’s writing while I’m rewriting
which is running like on a treadmill.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.700
You can rewrite after you’re done.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.330
But you don’t rewrite…one time I had a
job and the contract stated they wanted to

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.160
see every ten pages.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.570
And I was like “That’s a deal breaker
for me.”

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.289
And so I told me attorney I said “I don’t
want that.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:29.940
You hired me to write the script.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:30.940
Let me write the script.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.130
That’s what a rewrites about.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.850
You won’t know in ten pages.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.230
It’s like running on a tread mill.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.630
That’s for me a habit that I don’t do.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:44.629
It’s tempting because when you get bored
and you scroll back and you’re like “Oh…I

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:46.110
wonder what those…?”

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.510
And then you realize the day is gone and you
should be on page 15 and I’m on 3 because

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.410
I really worked on those 3.”

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.300
That’s what a rewrite time is for.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.649
Another bad habit…let me see?

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:00.649
Well, fear.

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:04.010
I don’t know if that’s a habit.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.079
There’s a lot of fear involved in writing.

00:01:06.079 --> 00:01:09.090
You’re sitting there with the blank page
that you have to fill.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:14.619
And like I said before you can alleviate that
by having to sell a treatment which is sort

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:22.460
of your buddy and your roadmap and you won’t
sit there and waste time.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:27.189
Procrastination is another terrible one and
I’m guilty of it as well.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:31.340
A bad habit and there is always something
to distract you and then like I say when the

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.539
writing gets difficult as it does, it’s
the easiest time to run away from it.

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:42.569
And I found that even when you sit with it,
like I think Raymond Chandler said he sat

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:47.070
at his desk every day at the same time if
anything happened or it didn’t.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:56.729
It’s that constant repetition which writing
is, it’s not waiting for my Muse.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.289
I still have to…I mean the last job…I
was procrastinating but I had three pages

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:08.469
a day which was a light schedule but I found
myself on the Internet looking up stuff and

00:02:08.469 --> 00:02:11.349
procrastination is horrible.

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:12.349
What are we at now, three?

00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:13.410
Film Courage: You’re at 3.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.410
Mark Sanderson.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.410
I’m at 3.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:23.450
Number 4…oh, I’ve got to come up with
2 other ones, bad habits?

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.450
Personal habits?

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.450
Film Courage: Screenwriting.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.080
Mark Sanderson: Screenwriting habits.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:35.069
Overwriting, something I don’t do but I
warn against it because when I consult on

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:40.379
scripts I read that they are describing the
wallpaper, you know you have to know what

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:42.680
you need to put in and what you need to leave
out.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:56.370
I find a lot of beginning writers hang on
so desperately and that’s how you develop

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:04.900
a 120 page script and you look through and
go cut this out.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:11.110
They don’t want to see all of this description
and that’s a technique to know when to stay

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.220
out of the way which is important.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.250
I think for writers you’ve got to know when
to put your imprint on it but stay out of

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.860
the way.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.129
And so we’re up to 4 now?

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:23.129
Film Courage: 4.

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:24.129
But if you….yeah…

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:27.099
Mark Sanderson: 5…what’s the other one?

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:28.930
I don’t know.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.590
I can’t think of a fifth.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.040
Film Courage: That’s okay.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.230
So when you catch yourself procrastinating
do you sometimes just say “Alright, I’m

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.459
just going to do it for a little bit and get
it out of my system.

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:44.590
Or what do you do to be your own taskmaster.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:50.310
Mark Sanderson: Well I used to have a writing
partner, so it helps to have someone go “Hey!”

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.489
Or you’re together because it’s both of
your collective time.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:55.439
You’re not wasting somebody else’s time.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:58.129
But when you’re alone, you can waste as
much time as you want.

00:03:58.129 --> 00:04:01.159
“It’s just my time.”

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:07.450
But I usually realize that I’m doing it
and then snap back into “Oh, I should be…”

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:08.620
It’s tough, distractions.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:14.290
That’s why you have to go somewhere and
write if you have an office or they have office

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:19.739
spaces now that you can rent and it’s a
short-term thing per day.

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.270
And one time a producer hired me on assignment
and he paid for that office space so I could

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:30.030
go there and it was something about when I
go to the gym, you made a commitment to take

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.290
that trip to get there and park and so you’re
going to be productive rather than working

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.710
at home where there’s a lot of distractions.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.020
If you have a family somebody knocking at
the door, the phone ringing, other times I’ve

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.170
worked at the library where it’s quiet.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:52.870
So work environment is also very important
to fight off the forces of procrastination.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:57.500
Film Courage: Yeah, the whole co-working culture
is very interesting.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.259
What about overwriting, I’m wondering if
you can give an example of something that

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:06.270
is overwritten versus shortening it down?

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:12.770
Mark Sanderson: Well mainly it’s the descriptions
in the script but also over writing the dialogue

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:17.170
where you can go through in a piece where
you can say “You don’t need that beginning

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.250
sentence.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:25.979
You are saying the same thing in repetition
two lines from now.

00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:31.080
Concise down to the point of…or overwriting
where a character says something and it would

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.720
be better if they just had a reaction.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.860
I always try to air on the side of let the
actors act and emote rather than say something.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:44.160
If you see someone’s eyes 60 feet on the
screen that’s going to say a lot more than

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.470
a line of dialogue, you know.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.389
So cut, cut, cut, cut, cut.

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:51.340
They say cut the dialogue and then cut it
20% more than that.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.870
And you’re like “Oh, lean!”

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:57.849
And no one wants to because, but I’ve read
screenplays that read like a stage play and

00:05:57.849 --> 00:06:00.449
you’re like that’s no what this is.

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:03.770
Or scenes that every director I’ve worked
with said give me something to shoot where

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:08.900
the scene is just flat and two people sitting
and Quentin Tarantino can do it with interesting

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:15.169
dialogue with 10 pages of dialogue but two
characters sitting in a dinner scene, the

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:17.680
directors I’ve worked with they hate it
they are like “What are we doing?”

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.169
It’s just this, this, this, you know?

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:29.710
And plus the food would be spoiled, it takes
a long time to film it.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:34.310
And overwriting the descriptions like I said
before, describing things that are not your

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:40.460
job you just set the scene and if it’s a
castle you don’t have to say all of the

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:44.889
intricacies of the interior of the castle
or there is a Knight in Shining Armor or there’s

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:47.080
a tapestry.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.470
The set designer will do that.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:57.770
You have to stand back and in a way you have
to really know what you need to put in that’s

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.990
necessary and what is unnecessary.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.680
And I think a lot of beginning writers don’t
know that (that fine line) and they just put

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:04.759
everything in.

00:07:04.759 --> 00:07:10.900
Film Courage: And if someone were to read
it and it drags, would they know?

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.849
Let’s suppose it’s their work and they
think “Oh, no…but I’m describing this

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:18.449
interaction between two people and the tension
and it’s great.

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:21.789
But then an outside person is looking at it
saying “this is dragging.”

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:24.330
Mark Sanderson: Yeah, cut to the chase of
what’s going on.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.289
Less is always more, you know what I mean?

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:34.750
And a producer that I work with, he caught
the repetition in dialogue and I didn’t

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.500
even see it, you are saying the same thing
again here and we don’t need it and I’m

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:38.530
like “Oh, my gosh, you’re right.”

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:45.319
And he’s a big anti-repetition producer
and I’ve never had that before, like a producer

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:50.250
who would point out those specifics and I’m
like “I’ve got this bad habit now of not

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.380
even seeing it.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:57.370
And so now I’m very cognizant of that when
I work with him and in general that we only

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:03.889
have so much time on the screen and so many
pages and if you don’t like the constraints,

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:05.090
write a novel.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.430
You can do whatever you want.

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:12.120
But if you like the screenplay format you
have to have these dense packed scenes with

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.770
subtext but not go on for 6-8 pages.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.691
There’s a rhythm to it, there’s a feel.

00:08:17.691 --> 00:08:23.080
Like you say you start reading that the script
is overwritten, you just now everything is

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:31.080
described and the writer is so afraid to let
go that their vision or ideas wont come through

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.500
on the page.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.660
Well, once they take that script and make
it, yeah.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:42.980
But your imprint is the script so all these
other little particulars are not going to

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:43.890
help when it’s overwritten.

